course: arts
lesson: 7
title: How a song is made: writing, recording, producing
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A song usually grows in steps: someone writes a melody (the tune you can sing) and lyrics (the words), it gets a rough first recording called a demo, then it is recorded properly, mixed and mastered.
explain: There is no one right order: some writers start with words, some with a tune, a chord pattern or a beat, and many hits began as one line or a few notes hummed into a phone.
explain: In a studio each part goes on its own track, so one singer can record the main tune and then five harmonies on top; picture a girl singing in a choir of herself.
explain: The producer shapes how the record sounds, choosing instruments and making the big decisions; mixing balances the tracks, and mastering is the final polish so it sounds right on phones, speakers and radio.
explain: People argue in their own words: "quid" is British slang for pounds, "extra" means over the top, in Ireland "brutal" means very bad and "grand" means fine, and in the West Country "my lover" is just a friendly way to say "my dear".
fact: Some songwriters start with words, some with a tune or chords, some with a beat; there is no single correct way.
fact: A music producer guides how a recording sounds, choosing instruments, helping arrange the parts, running sessions and deciding on the final sound.
fact: Auto-Tune is software that can fix slightly-off notes or, turned up, give a robotic effect on purpose; Cher's 1998 song "Believe" made that effect famous.
fact: Many skilled singers use Auto-Tune as a tool or a style, so using it does not prove someone cannot sing; the fairest way to judge a singer is to hear them sing live.
fact: Multitrack recording puts each part on its own track, so one singer can record many layers of harmony.
fact: Billie Eilish and her brother Finneas reportedly recorded much of her debut album on a computer in a small bedroom at home, and it won major awards.
fact: Many pop songs are written by teams, and everyone whose work is used gets a credit, an official mention.
fact: Sampling means using a short piece of an old recording in a new song; it is common in hip-hop and pop, and usually needs permission, called clearing the sample.
fact: A demo is a rough first recording made to show the idea of a song; it does not need to sound perfect.
fact: The hook is the catchiest bit of a song, often in the chorus; writing a good hook takes real skill.
fact: Mixing sets the volume and sound of each track so they fit; mastering is the final polish of the finished mix; they are separate steps.
fact: AI tools can make music from a text request; who owns AI music and whether the tools were trained fairly are argued about, and in 2024 major record labels reportedly sued some AI music companies.
exercise: Lily asks whether you write the words or the tune first. What is true? | Either, songwriters work in many different orders | Always the words first, because that's the rule for songs | Always the tune first, words come last | Neither, songs are written by computers
exercise: Emre thinks a producer "just pushes buttons". What does a producer do? | Shapes how the recording sounds and makes big decisions | Only presses the record button, then goes home for the day | Sells tickets for the singer's concerts | Writes the reviews in the newspaper
exercise: Josh says singers who use Auto-Tune can't really sing. What is true? | Many good singers use it as a tool or a style | Auto-Tune proves a singer has no talent | Auto-Tune can make anyone at all into a great singer | Only bad singers are allowed to use it
exercise: Which song made the robotic Auto-Tune effect famous? | Cher's "Believe" from 1998 | "Shchedryk" from the 1910s | Thriller from 1982 | "Dynamite" from 2020
exercise: Anya hears five voices and it is the same girl. How? | Multitrack recording layers her parts on separate tracks | Five sisters who all sound exactly the same as each other | A microphone that splits one voice into five | She sang so loudly it echoed five times
exercise: Zara says hits need a massive studio and millions of quid. What is true? | Some award-winning albums were made in bedrooms | No song was ever made outside a big studio | You need a million pounds just to record a single note | Home recordings are not allowed in the charts
exercise: Raj sees seven songwriters on one song. Why? | Many pop songs are written by teams who all get credit | Six of them must have stolen the song from the real writer | Each writer wrote a different song by mistake | Songs are only allowed with seven writers
exercise: Tom hears an old 1970s bit inside a new rap song. What is it called? | Sampling, which is legal with permission | Stealing, which is always illegal | Mixing, the final polish of a song | Mastering, which only songs from the 1970s ever need
exercise: What is clearing a sample? | Getting permission from the owners of the original | Deleting the sample from the song | Making the sample louder in the mix | Cleaning the dust off the old record before you play it again
exercise: Dylan's demo sounds "brutal". Is that a problem? | No, a demo only needs to show the idea | Yes, a demo must sound perfect | Yes, bad demos can never become songs | No, because demos are never heard by anyone
exercise: Liu Yang keeps singing one short line. What is that part called? | The hook, the catchiest part | The bridge, the quiet middle part | The intro, the very first notes | The credit, the list of writers
exercise: Kai asks the difference between mixing and mastering. What is it? | Mixing balances the tracks; mastering polishes the finished whole | They are two names for the same step | Mastering comes first, then mixing | Mixing is only for the drums, and mastering is only for the voices
exercise: Theo says, "Just get AI to write it, job done." What is fair to say? | He would not learn the skill, and AI music ownership is argued about | AI music is exactly the same as writing your own song from scratch | AI tools are banned everywhere by law | Nobody has ever argued about AI music
exercise: What is a melody? | The tune you can sing | The words written for a song | The steady beat of the drums | The list of people who helped
exercise: What happened with AI music companies in 2024? | Major record labels reportedly sued some of them | They were given every single Grammy award that year | They were bought by Eurovision | Nothing has ever been reported about them
exercise: Who recorded much of Billie Eilish's debut album with her in a bedroom? | Her brother Finneas | The Beatles | Cher | Her music teacher
test: Your mate goes, "catchy songs are lazy, anyone could write one." What is true? | Writing a good hook takes a lot of skill | Catchy songs are written by accident, without any trying | Only lazy people write catchy songs | Hooks are always copied from adverts
test: What would happen if a band only mixed a song but never mastered it? | It might sound uneven on phones, speakers and radio | It would sound exactly the same anyway, so mastering is a waste | It would be twice as long | The band would lose all their songs
test: Tom says, "Any sample is stealing, full stop." Which is fair? | Sampling with permission is legal and common | He's right, every sample is illegal | Samples are fine without asking anyone | Only old songs can be sampled
test: A cartoon shows a producer asleep while the band does everything. What does it get wrong? | Producers make many big decisions about the sound | Nothing, producers really do nothing | Producers are only there to make tea | Producers play every instrument in every song while the band watches
test: Josh in Toronto says, "Auto-Tune means they can't sing, eh." What is the best way to judge a singer? | Hear them sing live | Count how many effects they use | Check how many followers they have | Look at their clothes
test: Raj says seven writers on one song is "well extra" (over the top). What is the truth? | Team writing is normal, and everyone who helps gets credit | One song can only have one real writer, so the rest are just helpers | The other six are fake names | Seven writers means the song is bad
test: Lily's uncle says "Depends, my lover." What does he mean? | It depends on the song, dear | He loves the song she wrote for him | She should never write songs again | Words always come first, before the tune
test: Dylan says his demo sound is "brutal but the tune's grand". What is he saying? | The sound is bad but the tune is good | The sound is great and the tune is bad | Both the sound and the tune are perfect | He wants to delete the whole thing now
test: Zara says only rich bands can make songs. What would be a good reply? | Many songs are made at home with a computer | She's right, songs need millions of pounds and a famous studio | Bedrooms are too small for music | Only studios have microphones
test: Anya says, "Five voices means five singers, every time." What is wrong? | One singer can record many layers on separate tracks | Nothing, five voices always means five people | Voices can never be recorded twice | Studios only allow one voice per song
test: Theo uses AI for every song and never writes himself. What might he miss? | The skills of songwriting | Nothing, AI teaches you everything | The chance to be sued by himself | His own voice, which AI steals
exam: Identify the principal function of a music producer in the making of a recording. | The direction of the sound of the recording, including major decisions of arrangement | The sale of tickets for the performances of the artist | The operation of the recording button and no other duty whatsoever during the sessions | The publication of critical reviews of the recording
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately distinguishes mixing from mastering? | Mixing balances the individual tracks, whereas mastering polishes the completed mix | The two terms denote one and the same procedure | Mastering precedes mixing in every instance | Mixing concerns percussion and mastering concerns voices exclusively
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the incorporation of a portion of an earlier recording into a new composition. | It is lawful and common when permission is obtained from the owners | It is unlawful in every circumstance whatsoever | It requires no permission of any kind | It is permitted only for recordings made before 1970
exam: Determine the most accurate characterisation of the use of pitch-correction software by a vocalist. | It does not of itself establish an inability to sing | It establishes conclusively an absence of talent | It renders any person a singer of the first rank | It is forbidden to all professional vocalists
